      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: what did kūdalu mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ಬೆರಳು",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಬೆರಳು (beraḷu) — \"a finger\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Tamil has விரல் (viral), Malayalam വിരൽ, Telugu వేలు (vēlu) — and Kannada opens the word with b- where its sisters have v-."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That is the second regular exchange this book has shown you, the one already visible in ಬರೆ, 'to write', where the sisters again have a v. One family, two swaps: p- for h-, v- for b-. Between them they account for a great many words that look unrelated at first glance and are nothing of the kind."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಬೆರಳು (beraḷu) is native Dravidian, one word with Tamil viral, Telugu vēlu and Malayalam viral (DEDR 5409). DEDR gives Kannada beral, beraḷ: the old v- has become b-."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four. A leg, a tooth, hair, a finger."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
