          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಹೊಟ್ಟೆ (hoṭṭe) — \"the stomach, the belly\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Telugu has పొట్ట (poṭṭa) for the same thing, the p being the older shape and Kannada's h the newer one. Tamil went its own way here and reaches for வயிறு (vayiṟu), a word from another root entirely — a reminder that the sisters agree often, not invariably."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five body words, and three of them — ಹಲ್ಲು (hallu), ಹೊಟ್ಟೆ (hoṭṭe), and the fruit from the chapter before — carry the same h into the same first slot."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಹೊಟ್ಟೆ (hoṭṭe) is the older Kannada poṭṭe, \"belly\", with the p- turned into h-. DEDR 4494 lists it with Telugu poṭṭa, and compares Prakrit poṭṭa, \"belly\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five more body words: a leg, a tooth, hair, a finger, the belly."
            }
